            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-liaoning-2024-12-famous-schools-joint:grammar-fill:1",
            "text": "For many children in the U. S., going back to school means more than returning to classes. It means getting back to student organizations interested in environmental sustainability, where everything from stopping food waste and choosing products made from organic material to organizing campaigns for cleaner oceans is involved."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-liaoning-2024-12-famous-schools-joint:grammar-fill:2",
            "text": "April Peebler is the executive director（执行董事）of Heirs To Our Oceans, a California-based group meant for ocean conservation. She said that more and more young people are actively participating in sustainability issues. Fifteen-year-old Hannah is one of them. When she was 10 years old, she and some friends started an anti-plastic campaign on the Internet. They urged Americans to tell the restaurant Dunkin’ Donuts about their demand for recyclable cups."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-liaoning-2024-12-famous-schools-joint:grammar-fill:3",
            "text": "Perhaps the most famous teenage environmental activist is 16-year-old Greta Thunberg of Sweden, who motivated others to launch climate-change strikes around the world last year. Also enthusiastic about fighting food waste and hunger in America, the girl now acts as a consultant of the Food Recovery Network, a student-led movement. Students at colleges connect with college dining halls, and local shelters to make sure that surplus food is given to those in need. Regina Anderson is the group’s executive director. She says students have recovered 3.9 million pounds of food to date."
          }
